From 13b4960c067437c553e5c885f635e2cf3fe5c416 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Young <davidy@funkypenguin.co.nz>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 09:16:11 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] feat: add REPLAY_BYPASS_HOSTS for the built-in Newznab client

Hosts named in REPLAY_BYPASS_HOSTS are requested directly rather than through
the forwarding endpoints configured by BUILTIN_NAB_REPLAY_ENDPOINT and
BUILTIN_NZB_REPLAY_DOWNLOAD_URL.

Applies to both paths: the search call matches on the upstream host, and the
NZB fetch matches on the enclosure's own host, which is not always the same
host.

Entry forms:

  host        exact match
  .domain     the domain and any subdomain
  *.domain    subdomains only, not the domain itself

Entries are bare hostnames, optionally with a port, and may be separated by
commas, semicolons or whitespace in any mix.

Anything carrying a scheme, path, query, fragment or credentials is rejected
rather than trimmed down to the host it appears to name. Separators are
applied first, so a pasted URL cannot be read back reliably: a semicolon
inside a password splits one value into several entries, and
`https://user:p;*.com;ss@host.example/` would otherwise contribute `*.com` as
a rule of its own.

For the same reason a wildcard needs at least two labels. `*`, `*.`, `.`,
`*.com` and `.com` are all dropped: the list selects individual hosts and has
no catch-all form.

Comparison ignores case, port, IPv6 brackets, the trailing DNS root dot and
IDN spelling on both sides. Three IPv6 cases a naive host:port split gets
wrong are handled explicitly: a bracketed literal must not be cut at its
first colon; an unbracketed one has no port to strip, and cutting `::1` there
yields the empty string; and a scope id is dropped, since URL.host drops it
and an entry keeping one could never match its own destination.

Unset or empty leaves behaviour unchanged.
